  "query_preview": "..For remainder of Sunday through Monday...Low \npressure located over southwest Arizona Sunday afternoon will \nslowly track northeastward across the state, weakening as it \ngoes. The low will deliver scattered showers and perhaps a stray \nafternoon thunderstorm each day. However, there are a few factors\nthat will limit precipitation amounts with this low. First, the \nlow is weak and will grow weaker. Second, the low-level flow and \nthus orographic lift will be minimal. Third, mainly cloudy skies \nwill limit daytime heating and associated instability. As a \nresult, generally light precipitation is anticipated with totals \nranging from trace amounts to 0.20 inches through Monday (the \nhighest mountain peaks could see as much as 0.50 inches). In any \ncase, this is a warm system so snow levels are expected to remain \nprimarily above 11,000 feet. Light and variable winds on Sunday \nwill turn more southerly on Monday as another Pacific low \napproaches. There will be a cooling influence from the light \nprecipitation and clouds with high temperatures dropping by around\n5 degrees on Sunday and Monday compared to Saturday.\n\nOn Tuesday...The current weak low will move to the northeast \nin response to a stronger low pressure system dropping southward \nfrom the Gulf of Alaska. Moisture will continue to linger across\nArizona in advance of this stronger low with slight chances for \nshowers or a stray thunderstorm continuing. Activity will mainly\nbe confined to the afternoon and early evening associated with\ninstability induced by daytime heating. Southerly winds will \nstrengthen to 10-20 mph as the low draws closer to Arizona. High \ntemperatures will warm a bit as southerly flow pushes a mild air \nmass across Arizona.\n\nFrom Wednesday onward...Forecast models continue to show the \nPacific low diving southward then moving across the western \nstates including Arizona, tapping into moisture of subtropical (or\neven tropical) origins. The result, widespread showers and likely\na few thunderstorms are forecast, especially on Wednesday and \nThursday. The most substantial moisture will likely move across \nArizona from about Flagstaff eastward where local rainfall totals \nranging from 1 to 3 inches will be possible. The area of the \nhighest rainfall totals includes the White Mountains, Mogollon \nRim, Navajo Nation and Gila County. Further west looking at\nlighter amounts of 0.25-0.75 inches. The snow level will remain \nelevated for much of the storm due to the subtropical to tropical \nnature of the moisture. The snow level could drop as low as 8500 \nfeet as the coldest temperatures aloft move over Arizona on Friday\nand Saturday (when shower chances will be rapidly diminishing). \nAlso, Pressure gradients will strengthen on Wednesday and Thursday\nwith south to southwest winds of 15-25 mph gusting to 35 mph \ndeveloping. Later on Friday and on Saturday a weak low will linger\nover Arizona and would not be suprised if at least a slight \nchance of showers lingers. High temperatures will be on the \ndecrease under the influence of perception, clouds and cooler air \nassociated with the low.",
